    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Taxation",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4577\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act in relation to tax assessments for certain\nimproved properties affected by Superstorm Sandy\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:  Creates a tax exemption for owner\noccupied residential property in municipalities affected by Superstorm\nSandy.  Provides authority to local assessors to implement a phased\nin, graduated assessment increase over an 8 year period to property\nowners who were victims of Superstorm Sandy and had to complete\nsignificant repairs, reconstruction and in some cases build entire new\nhomes in the aftermath of Superstorm Sandy.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 (a)creates a tax exemption for owner occupied residential\nproperty in municipalities and school districts affected by Superstorm\nSandy which, after a public hearing, have adopted a local law or\nresolution to grant the exemption, and filed it with the commissioner\nof taxation and finance and the assessor of the municipality.\n\nSection 1(b)lists requirements for eligibility.\n\nSection 1 (c) specifies the duration of the exemption; (i) defines\n\"exemption base\"; (ii) discusses conditions requiring the recomputing\nof the \"exemption base\"; (iii) specifies how the exemption shall be\napplied.\n\nSection 1 (d) specifies under what circumstances an exemption shall be\ngranted.\n\nSection 1 (e) specifies how the exemption shall be filed and the\nduration of the exemption.\n\nSection 1 (f) specifies the details of application approval. Section 1\n\n(g) defines \"primary residence.\"\n\nSection 1 (h) specifies under what conditions the exemption shall\ncease.\n\nSection 1 (i) specifies that no local law or resolution shall reduce\nor repeal an exemption that has been granted.\n\nSection 2 sets out the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  In the aftermath of Superstorm Sandy, many\nstorm-impacted homeowners were required to do extensive repairs and\nreconstruction so that they could remain in or return to their homes.\nAs a result for many homeowners, their property. assessments have\nincreased substantially and they are now faced with the financial\nburdens of loan payments for the reconstruction in addition to\nincreased property taxes.  This legislation allows for an exemption\nwhich provides a graduated tax increase over an eight year period for\ncertain eligible Superstorm Sandy victims.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New bill.\n\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: To be determined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect immediately and shall\napply to exemption applications made prior to the first of March next\nsucceeding the date on which this act shall have become a law and no\nlater than the first of March two thousand eighteen, and any exemption\nonce granted shall continue for a period of up to eight prospective\ntax years as set forth in this act.\n\n",
